# Sprint 3 — Teacher Portal

## Overview

Sprint 3 gives the AI marking engine built in Sprint 2 its first real front door: a teacher-facing portal. Nothing here duplicates marking logic — every feature calls the existing `Paper`, `Memorandum`, and `MarkingResult` models and the OpenRouter/Qwen2.5-VL call from Sprint 2.

## Goals

- Let a teacher author memorandums through a real UI, not just admin
- Let a teacher upload and mark a paper for a specific student, and see the structured result
- Give teachers a history view of what they've marked
- Let a teacher create assignments and study materials, ready for students to see in Sprint 4
- Keep the MVP's data model simple: no class/roster structure yet

## What was built

- **Memorandum authoring UI** — teacher-facing form to create and list memorandums (question, expected answer, marks available per question), replacing the Sprint 2 admin-only entry. A teacher can type it in, or photograph a memorandum they already have on paper and let the vision model transcribe it into the form to review before saving
- **Mark a paper (teacher-facing)** — teacher uploads a paper photo, selects a memorandum, selects the student from a searchable dropdown, and submits to the Sprint 2 engine; the structured result (score + per-question feedback) displays on screen
- **Graceful failure state** — a `failed` marking status shows a clear, plain-language message in the UI, not a blank page or a stack trace, plus a one-press retry that re-marks the stored photo (withheld for failures a retry cannot fix, such as an empty account)
- **Marking history** — a list view of papers this teacher has marked, with status and score, most recent first
- **`Assignment` model + posting UI** — title, instructions, linked memorandum, optional due date, created by the teacher; the instructions can be typed or transcribed from a photo, same as memorandums; visible to all students for now (no class scoping — see known limitations)
- **`StudyMaterial` model + posting UI** — title, plain-text content, created by the teacher; same scope simplification as assignments
- **Access control** — all views reject non-teacher roles at the view level, consistent with Sprint 1's pattern, and every list is scoped to the teacher who owns the records
- **Tests** — unauthorized role rejection, the marking flow and the transcription flow (AI calls mocked, no real API quota spent in tests), and persistence of assignments and study materials

## Implementation notes

Decisions worth having on record for later sprints and demo prep.

- **A separate `classroom` app.** Assignments and study materials live in
  `classroom/`, not `marking/`. They are not marking — a study material has no
  memorandum at all — and Sprint 4 reads both from the student side. Folding
  them into `marking` would have made that app about two unrelated things.

- **`Paper` gained a `student` field.** A `Paper` now records the learner whose
  work it is, separately from `submitted_by` (who uploaded it). A teacher marking
  a class set is not the author of any of it, and a mark that is not attached to
  a learner cannot reach them or their parent in Sprint 6. The field is nullable
  so Sprint 2's JSON endpoint still works; the teacher form requires it, and a
  learner submitting their own homework in Sprint 4 fills it in automatically.

- **One submission path.** `marking/submissions.py` is the single route from an
  uploaded file to a marked paper (validate → compress → store → mark). Both the
  teacher page and Sprint 2's JSON endpoint go through it, so the two cannot
  drift apart.

- **Retry button and list views, beyond the brief.** The failure page offers a
  retry that re-marks the already-stored photo, so a teacher does not have to
  find and re-photograph the paper; it is withheld for failures a retry cannot
  fix (empty account, withdrawn model). Assignments and study materials each got
  a list view, because a teacher who posts something with no confirmation it
  saved will reasonably post it again.

- **Ownership is the access boundary.** With no class/roster structure, every
  list and detail view is scoped to the teacher who created the record, and
  memorandums are scoped per-teacher everywhere (the mark and assignment
  dropdowns, and the "you need a memorandum first" prompts). Another teacher
  opening your paper gets a 404. Stricter than the brief asked, and easy to
  relax when rosters arrive.

- **Photograph-to-transcribe for memorandums and assignments.** A teacher can
  upload a photo instead of typing. It reuses the Sprint 2 image pipeline and the
  same vision model, but asks for plain text rather than JSON, and — unlike
  marking — the result is never saved on its own. It pre-fills the form for the
  teacher to review and edit first, because OCR misreads and a silently altered
  marking guide is worse than one typed by hand. The source photo is not stored;
  it is only a means of getting text. If the model is unreachable or rate
  limited, the teacher sees a plain message and the typing fields are still
  right there. Lives in `marking/transcription.py`.

- **The interface.** A single stylesheet (`static/css/isgela.css`), no framework
  and no web-font download, aimed at a teacher who may be in their sixties: 18px
  base text, 48px minimum tap targets, status carried by words as well as
  colour, visible keyboard focus, and reduced-motion honoured. The small
  `static/js/isgela.js` is optional progressive enhancement (type-to-filter the
  learner list, confirm the chosen photo, show progress while marking runs);
  every page works with it blocked.

## Setup additions this sprint

No new environment variables or dependencies — this sprint builds UI and models on top of Sprint 2's existing engine and credentials.

Before running this sprint: confirm a spend cap is set on the OpenRouter account, since this sprint puts the marking engine behind a UI that will be clicked repeatedly by testers and, eventually, judges.

## Definition of done

- [x] Teacher can create a memorandum through the UI
- [x] Teacher can upload and mark a paper for a specific student and see the structured result on screen
- [x] A failed marking attempt shows a clear error state, not a crash
- [x] Teacher can view their marking history
- [x] Teacher can create an assignment linked to a memorandum
- [x] Teacher can create a study material item
- [x] All views reject non-teacher roles at the view level
- [x] Tests cover unauthorized access, the mocked marking flow, and assignment/study material persistence

## Out of scope for this sprint

Student-facing display of assignments or study materials (Sprint 4), facial recognition attendance (Sprint 5), parent notifications (Sprint 6), the cross-subject progress dashboard (Sprint 7).

## Known limitations

- **No class/roster structure yet.** Teachers select a student from a
  full list when marking a paper or creating an assignment, rather
  than from their own class roster. Assignments and study materials
  are visible to all students rather than scoped to a specific class.
  This keeps the MVP's data model simple within the hackathon
  timeline; a production version would add a `School`/`Class`
  structure so teachers only see and interact with their own
  students. This is a deliberate scope decision, not an oversight.
- **Parent-student linking is still self-declared and unverified**
  (carried over from Sprint 1) — unchanged by this sprint.

## Next up — Sprint 4

Build the student portal: view study materials and assignments, and submit homework via camera scan, which calls the same Sprint 2 marking engine used here.